B.LD=new A.apf("Privacy policy","Last updated: 8 November 2026\n\n1. Data Collection\nWe store your email, display name and avatar in our database (Supabase). Group documents are stored on our servers, and database rules decide who can open them. They are not end-to-end encrypted: for that, send a One open link. A PDF you open from another app stays on your phone. Your own chat stays on your phone, or in your own Google Drive if you connect it, where we access only files NO SUS created.\n\n2. Burn notes and files\nThey are encrypted on your device before upload, and the key travels in the link, not to us. Only if you ask for a 6-digit code is a copy of the key kept on our servers: the code lasts up to 10 minutes, and the copy is deleted within 10 minutes after the code is used or expires. The website works the same way. The encrypted content is deleted after it is opened or when it expires. If you are signed in, our server knows which account sent each link until it is opened or expires, so it can tell you it was opened, never who opened it. Turn that off in Settings \u2192 Notifications \u2192 Link opened.\n\n3. Audit Logs\nTo help trace leaks, we record events such as file openings, link views and blocked screenshot attempts, with the account email and time. Group members can see the activity of their group. These records are deleted with your account.\n\n4. Product Analytics\nWe record a small set of product events (app opened, first-run setup started/completed, sign-up completed, first document uploaded or viewed, and notification permission outcomes) against your account so we can see where the app is confusing. These records never contain document names, document contents, group names, or message text.\n\n5. Notifications\nIf you enable push notifications we store a device token so messages can be delivered. Notification text never includes a document name or its contents. Signing out releases that device token.\n\n6. Data Deletion\nYou may delete your account at any time. Account deletion removes your profile, profile picture, memberships, every file you uploaded (including files in groups other people still use) and the group activity records of what you did. Product analytics records and feedback are kept without any link to your account.\n\n7. Open on any screen\nWhen your phone and a computer share a chat, they may try to connect directly to send files. To find a path, each side asks public STUN servers run by Cloudflare (stun.cloudflare.com) and Google (stun.l.google.com) for the address its network shows the internet. Those servers see the public IP address of each device. They see no session, note or file. When a direct path is blocked, which is common on mobile data in India, the connection may go through a Cloudflare TURN relay. A relay sees both devices' public IP addresses and ports, when the connection is open and how much data passes through it. It can't read files, file names or notes, because the connection is encrypted between your phone and that computer with keys checked inside the sealed session. If neither a direct path nor a relay works, files go through our server, sealed between your phone and that computer.",1,"privacy")
B.LE=new A.apf("Terms of service","Last updated: October 2026\n\n1. What NO SUS does\nNO SUS keeps your own chat, the notes and files you send yourself, on your phone, or in a NO SUS folder in your own Google Drive if you connect it. You can share files and notes with groups you start or join. You can send a link that opens once: it is encrypted on your phone first and the stored copy is deleted after the first open. You can send a watermarked link that shows the email the reader enters on every page, and revoke it. NO SUS shows no ads.\n\n2. Your content\nYou are responsible for what you upload and send. Do not use NO SUS to share things you have no right to share or to harass anyone. Content and accounts that break this can be removed. You can report content in a group from the app.\n\n3. Limits\nA watermark or a screenshot block cannot stop someone photographing a screen. NO SUS is provided as is: we are not responsible for network failures, lost content, or what a recipient does with what you send.\n\n4. Changes\nWhen these terms change, the new text appears here.",0,"terms")
